3389: #  *** A PYTHON DICT LITERAL ACCEPTS A DUPLICATE KEY IN SILENCE, AND THE LAST ONE WINS.
3390: #  MEASURED ON THIS FILE, HOURS AFTER IT WAS WRITTEN. ***
3391: #
3392: #  Two rounds added `sgi_eaddr_probe.py` to MANIFEST independently -- the author of this
3393: #  checker, and the #446 round whose probe it was.  Both landed, four lines apart, and
3394: #  `len(MANIFEST)` reported ONE entry.  Nothing complained, because nothing can: by the
3395: #  time the module object exists the earlier entry is already gone.  Here the two agreed
3396: #  on CLASS, so the behaviour was identical and the only damage was a stale line.  Had
3397: #  they disagreed -- one DETECTOR, one TOOL -- the file would have carried a visible,
3398: #  reviewed, *tracked* classification that the checker silently did not use, and the
3399: #  filename tooth that refuses `*_probe.py` in class `tool` would have been reading the
3400: #  wrong entry while looking correct in the diff.
3401: #
3402: #  It has to be caught by re-reading THIS FILE'S SOURCE TEXT, because the data structure
3403: #  cannot represent the fault it suffers from.  A duplicate is a SETUP FAULT, not a
3404: #  finding: it exits before any wiring is derived, so it can never be confused with a
3405: #  probe that is genuinely unwired.
3406: #  The open() is INLINE rather than a call to read() below: this guard runs at import time,
3407: #  fifty lines before read() is defined, and the first draft died with a NameError proving it.
3408: #  It failed loudly, which is luck -- a guard placed in a branch would have failed silently.
3409: def _refuse_duplicate_keys():
3410:     with open(os.path.abspath(__file__), "r", encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as fh:
3411:         src = fh.read()
3412:     #  *** AN ANCHOR THAT NO LONGER MATCHES IS ITSELF A SETUP FAULT, NOT A REASON TO SKIP,
3413:     #  AND THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS GUARD DID `continue`. ***  A flagship review MEASURED
3414:     #  the consequence: `MANIFEST =  {` -- one extra space, valid Python, invisible to a
3415:     #  reader -- makes a literal find() miss, the guard skips, and a planted duplicate key
3416:     #  runs GREEN.  That is this file's own thesis turned on itself: a fault in a guard does
3417:     #  not fail loudly, it stops guarding.  The regex tolerates whitespace, and a miss is
3418:     #  now reported rather than shrugged at.
3419:     for name in ("MANIFEST", "EXEMPT"):
3420:         m = re.search(r"^%s\s*=\s*\{" % re.escape(name), src, re.M)

